\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two independent period blocks}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take two axes with distinct periods. Each block of the
[signed coordinate group](fdip-0164) has one axis, so no equal-period quarter-turn
exists. The coordinate group has order #{4}. Its
determinant-one part consists of the identity and the reversal
of both axes. The lift in \ref{fdip-016U} has order #{8}.
The operator #{f_1 f_2} projects to the paired reversal and
squares to #{-\bbI_S} by anticommutation. Its first #{4} powers
therefore give the whole inverse image of determinant one.
The reversal of both axes commutes with all coordinate changes,
while its two lifts anticommute with #{f_1}. The center upstairs
has just the two scalar signs, as in \ref{fdip-016V}.}
